> (they work on _every_ door, chest, box) in nethack.

Maybe so, but I've never heard "lock pick" and "skeleton key" used
interchangeably, and they call to my mind very different objects.

A lock pick is a long metal tool with a shaped end used in combination
with a tension wrench to pick open modern pin and tumbler locks.
Perhaps there's an older usage of the term "lock pick", but I don't
know it.

The term skeleton key, I believe, comes from the old-style bit and
barrel keys where one could file away most of the key (leaving only a
"skeleton" of the original key) and have it open a family of different
locks.  This has led to modern usage with two completely different
definitions: (1) master key; (2) any antique, bit and barrel key, even
one that can only open one lock.  The second definition, which seems
improper, may have stuck simply because those old bit and barrel keys
look vaguely "skeletal".  I always assumed that NetHack was using the
first definition, and that NetHack "skeleton keys" were just master
keys: normal looking keys that happened to open all doors in the
dungeon.

Anyway, what's most relevant to the NetHack simulated world is that
someone using a lock pick looks like someone trying to gain
unauthorized entry to a door.  In contrast, someone using a
skeleton/master key looks, even under fairly close scrutiny, just like
someone using a normal key to open a door legitimately.

As a result, I too found it surprising that opening a door with a
skeleton key could arouse the guards' suspicions.  At the very least,
the guard should say "Hey, that's not your room!" instead of "Hey,
stop picking that lock!"

But, then, I'm no locksmith, so maybe I'm all turned around on the
issue.

